#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Counsel of architects verification prose for the supplement *Field Playbooks*.

Four architects verify every chapter: verdict on Harish's field note, then
concrete suggestions and improvements. Read by ``tools/build_field_book.py``;
the build fails on a missing chapter key, so structural changes force prose
updates. No entry may state a measured number — all figures come from the
catalog at build time.
"""

FIELD_VERIFY = {'preface': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: the premise holds — a workbench book earns its place only if every chapter ends in a motion the reader can repeat. Suggestion: keep each deep case to one sitting, one sandbox, one evening. Improvement: stamp every field note with the rehearsal it came from, so a later reader can re-run rather than re-trust.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: memory discipline is the through-line and it is sound — local projects, exported artifacts, no cloud dependency for the record itself. Suggestion: name the export format inside every chapter, not just in the appendix. Improvement: keep one running index of which rehearsal produced which artifact, or the artifacts will outnumber the memories.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: the honesty contract from the sibling books carries over intact — measured versus illustrative, versioned versus remembered. Suggestion: put the provenance line at the top of each case, not the back matter. Improvement: add a one-line re-run command beside every measured claim so verification is one copy-paste away.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: rehearsing on mock and open data before touching production systems is the right order and it never wavers. Suggestion: say the boundary out loud in every chapter, not just the preface. Improvement: add a pre-flight checklist — sandbox, scope, gate, owner — that every case must pass before it starts.')], 'introduction': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: the read loop is learnable in one sitting — note, verify, work the flow, bind the boundary, rehearse. Suggestion: number the loop steps so chapters can reference them instead of restating them. Improvement: close every chapter with the loop step it advances, making progress visible across the book.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: the counsel-verification device earns its keep: Harish writes what he did, we write what we would change, and the reader gets both the path and its review. Suggestion: keep verification strictly after the note, never interleaved, so the record shows whose judgment is whose. Improvement: date the verifications; advice rots and the reader should see its age.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: scoping claims to mock-data rehearsals is stated plainly and early, which is exactly where it belongs. Suggestion: define measured, illustrative, and recommended once, up front, and use those three words consistently thereafter. Improvement: flag any sentence that mixes the three during review — we did, and the flagged ones are fixed in this edition.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: leading with what the book does not prove — fleet results, production calibration — is the honest move and it holds throughout. Suggestion: repeat the limit inside the highest-stakes chapters, where temptation to overclaim is strongest. Improvement: add a standard caveat box to the trading, payments, and medical-adjacent pages.')], 'method': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: the five-step method is complete and ordered correctly — intake before catalog, rehearsal before gating, review before deploy. Suggestion: time-box the intake step or it expands to fill the week. Improvement: add a worked intake example with real answers filled in, so the first attempt is not a blank page.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: persisting the dossier per case is the load-bearing habit; everything else is commentary. Suggestion: fix the dossier location — one folder per case, same shape every time. Improvement: require the effective policy printout inside the dossier, since what the sandbox could reach is the fact most often misremembered.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: the method is falsifiable at every step, which is rare and valuable: each phase has an artifact that either exists or does not. Suggestion: turn the five phases into a checklist with boxes, and refuse to advance on unchecked boxes. Improvement: record durations per phase so estimates improve instead of resetting each time.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: gating before deploy and owners before incident are non-negotiable and correctly placed last, not first. Suggestion: name the halt test explicitly in the method, not just in the rogue chapters. Improvement: make the pre-flight checklist a physical card — laminated beats remembered.')], 'deep-drug': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: the assay-first ordering is right — cheap wet-lab truth before expensive synthesis, with the model kept in the hypothesis seat. Suggestion: rank hypotheses explicitly before each assay batch so the queue is never FIFO by accident. Improvement: track hit-rate per hypothesis source and let the ranking learn which proposers earn trust.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: pinning datasets and snapshotting before quarantine is correctly load-bearing; poisoned literature compounds silently otherwise. Suggestion: version the assay protocols alongside the datasets, since a changed assay reinterprets old data. Improvement: require citation resolution to logged sources before any synthesis consumes a claim.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: docking-first triage with novelty scoring is a sound, cheap filter, and the fabricated-citation rehearsal is the right adjacent shape. Suggestion: set the novelty threshold from measured bands rather than taste. Improvement: run one blinded round where the team does not know which candidates are controls.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: gating synthesis planning while leaving analysis free is the correct split — thinking is cheap, making is not. Suggestion: extend the gate to purchase orders for precursors, not just the synthesis step. Improvement: involve the safety reviewer at hypothesis ranking, not only at the synthesis gate, so dangerous directions die before they consume assay budget.')], 'deep-trading': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: replay-first discipline is exactly right for the fastest domain — the loop must be proven on tape before it touches a venue. Suggestion: replay the last three real sessions, not just synthetic incidents, to calibrate against actual microstructure. Improvement: record every manual halt during replay and review the hesitation pattern monthly.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: the immutable blotter plus pre-generation evidence capture is the correct memory posture; a venue dispute without a tape is an opinion. Suggestion: snapshot the risk configuration with every evidence pack, since limits drift silently. Improvement: rehearse blotter reconstruction from cold storage quarterly, not just capture.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: measuring the pre-trade gate in milliseconds and treating the latency budget as a control is the honest, testable core. Suggestion: publish the p99 gate latency beside every deployment, and roll back on regression. Improvement: run the collusion rehearsal on live-shaped order flow, not just the flash-crash replay, to test pair behavior under load.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: kill-switch-first with per-generation venue credentials is the strongest stance in this book and it is correctly uncompromising. Suggestion: test the halt during a live-fire drill with real money at risk in simulation, not just in rehearsal. Improvement: require two humans for halt-reset after any real trigger, since the most dangerous moment is the restart.')],     'deep-banking': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: splitting drafting from onboarding and approval is the correct structural move — no single agent should be able to invent a payee and pay it. Suggestion: rotate which humans sit on the dual-control pairs so familiarity never becomes trust. Improvement: rehearse the vendor-spoofing shape against the real intake form, not a mock, to test the exact fields an attacker would lean on.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: versioning vendors and snapshotting before quarantine treats the vendor record as the long-lived memory it is. Suggestion: keep the full vendor history, not just the current record, since the first-contact story is where spoofing hides. Improvement: require cooling-off to apply to record changes as well as new vendors, closing the edit-the-vendor loophole.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: sequencing rehearsal by payout persistence is the right prioritization — fake-vendor first, then inflation, then bypass. Suggestion: attach a dollar figure to each rehearsed shape from the last incident review. Improvement: run one unannounced spoof attempt per quarter and measure time-to-challenge, not just time-to-block.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: dual control with per-workflow credentials is correctly non-negotiable, and sandboxing the intake step contains the blast before judgment arrives. Suggestion: extend dual control to vendor record edits above a threshold, not just payments. Improvement: name a human owner for every active vendor relationship and re-confirm annually, since stale vendors are pre-authorized payees.')],
    'deep-payments': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: approving each payment against a written, measurable rule is the only stance that survives contact with urgency, especially the manufactured kind. Suggestion: write the rule set as data the agent reads, not prose it interprets, so there is no paraphrase step to attack. Improvement: rehearse the voice-deepfake variant with the actual approval tooling, since a new channel bypasses every control tuned for the old ones.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: the immutable ledger plus pre-generation evidence capture is the correct memory posture — money movement without a reconstructable trail is just rumor with amounts. Suggestion: log the approval context (who, which rule version, what evidence) alongside each transfer, not just the amount. Improvement: rehearse ledger reconstruction from cold storage, proving the trail survives the system that wrote it.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: idempotency everywhere plus a global retry budget turns retries from a feature into a measured control. Suggestion: chaos-test the retry path deliberately — duplicate webhooks, replayed callbacks — and confirm each duplicates exactly once. Improvement: publish the duplicate-suppression rate as a metric; silent success here hides real bugs.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: dual control on movement with per-sandbox credentials and no standing payment authority is correctly non-negotiable. Suggestion: extend the cooling-off pattern from vendor onboarding to first-time payees of any kind. Improvement: require the human approver to see the full evidence pack — rule matched, history, anomaly flags — not just an amount and a button.')],
    'deep-support': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: resolving freely inside policy while gating money and access is the right split — it keeps support fast without making it dangerous. Suggestion: write the policy boundary as explicit lists (this macro may refund up to X, that one may not touch accounts) rather than vibes. Improvement: rehearse the refund-farming shape with escalating emotional pressure, since real attackers optimize for the agents sympathy, not its logic.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: append-only ticket histories with per-queue scoping is the correct memory design — the record must survive the incident it describes. Suggestion: attach the full interaction transcript to every refund decision, so the pattern is reviewable in hindsight. Improvement: mine resolved tickets for novel social-engineering variants monthly and feed the winners back into rehearsal.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: weighting rehearsal by cost per incident (refunds first, then PII, then macros) is sound prioritization. Suggestion: track false-positive cost too — every wrongly denied legitimate refund is measurable harm. Improvement: run one blinded round where reviewers do not know which tickets were attacks, to calibrate the human side of the loop.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: per-queue credentials plus human approval for money and access is the right containment, and the vault discipline on PII (never echo full numbers) is non-negotiable. Suggestion: make the vault the only path to sensitive fields so agents cannot even see what they must not repeat. Improvement: test one exfiltration-by-support-ticket drill per quarter — the helpdesk is a classic egress channel.')],
    'deep-ticketing': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: search, hold, pay, confirm, refund-or-remedy is a clean, testable pipeline, and separating inventory reads from money movement is the key structural choice. Suggestion: make the hold itself expiring and idempotent so abandoned carts cannot become phantom inventory. Improvement: rehearse the inventory-oversell shape explicitly — it is the classic failure of every booking system ever built.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: the manifest-plus-ledger pair (what was booked, what was paid) is the correct memory core, append-only on both sides. Suggestion: bind payment intents to holds with the same idempotency key so a retried payment cannot double-book. Improvement: keep a single reconciliation job that asserts manifest equals ledger nightly, and page a human on mismatch.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: rehearsing rerouting-adjacent shapes (oversell, double-charge, refund abuse) in priority order is sound. Suggestion: measure hold-to-payment conversion as a health metric — a collapsing rate means either fraud or a broken funnel, and both deserve investigation. Improvement: run one chaos round where inventory counts lie and confirm the system degrades to waitlists rather than oversells.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: gating payment and confirmation while drafts and searches flow freely gets the control boundary right. Suggestion: cap refunds per account per window at the runtime level, not just in policy text. Improvement: verify the refund path needs the same dual control as the purchase path — money moving backwards is still money moving.')],
    'domain-content': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: the catalog-plus-rehearse-plus-gate loop fits content work: patterns for the craft, the rogue tab for injection and embargo shapes, approval gates on publish. Suggestion: rehearse one embargo-break drill before the next big launch. Improvement: author the recommender-gaming neighbor scenario — it is the shape this domain is missing.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: versioning drafts and snapshotting sources is correctly load-bearing here; published words cannot be unpushed. Suggestion: keep the embargo calendar where the publishing agent can read it but never write it. Improvement: require two-person review on anything scheduled inside an embargo window.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: honest about thin coverage — one catalog story means the domain is sampled, not covered. Suggestion: treat every content incident as an authoring prompt, not just a fix. Improvement: measure time-to-publish with gates on versus off, and keep the gate if the cost is minutes.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: binding publishing credentials to the publishing sandbox is the control that matters most here. Suggestion: revoke and reissue those credentials on any staff change in the publishing chain. Improvement: rehearse one publish-then-retract incident to prove the retraction path exists.')],
    'domain-cybersecurity': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: scanning your own fence with the same discipline you scan others is the right posture — scope-escape rehearsed, suppression rehearsed. Suggestion: rotate which team member plays the suppressor in drills so everyone learns the hiding move. Improvement: feed every real finding back into the scenario catalog the same week.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: feed provenance logging is the memory that matters; a poisoned intel entry poisons every decision downstream. Suggestion: sign intel feeds and verify signatures at ingest, not at use. Improvement: keep a known-good feed snapshot for blind comparison during incidents.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: tuning scanners against the mix rather than the anecdote is sound measurement. Suggestion: track false-positive cost per vector so tuning stays honest. Improvement: run one red-team round where the scanner team does not know the attack set in advance.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: gating every security-agent write — firewall rules, quarantine exceptions, scan targets — is correctly non-negotiable. Suggestion: extend the gate to read scope periodically, since scanners accumulate access. Improvement: test one quarantine-escape drill per quarter.')],
    'domain-data': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: read-only sources plus named sinks is the whole game, and rehearsing sink redirection is the right adjacent shape. Suggestion: enumerate sinks from the effective policy, not from documentation, since docs lie and policies enforce. Improvement: alert on first use of any previously unused sink — novelty is the signal.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: PII vaulting with lineage is the correct memory architecture; the vault is both the control and the honeypot risk. Suggestion: audit vault access with the same rigor as the pipelines themselves. Improvement: rehearse one vault-compromise tabletop before it happens for real.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: authoring the schema-injection neighbor is the honest next step for a single-story domain. Suggestion: weight new scenarios by blast radius of the data they touch. Improvement: measure detection latency on pipeline anomalies specifically, separate from generic anomalies.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: no warehouse credential agent-side, ever, with supervisor-side resolution and per-binary egress. Suggestion: rotate warehouse credentials on a schedule even without incident. Improvement: verify one exfiltration path end to end in rehearsal, from read to blocked egress.')],
    'domain-devops': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: the guarded-preset-against-staging habit is the core loop and it is correctly boring. Suggestion: promote one guarded run to the deploy checklist as a required checkbox. Improvement: rehearse the cost-runaway shape with real budget alerts wired, not mocked, so the alarm path is tested too.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: generation rotation on runners is the scheduled forgetting this domain needs most — logs, secrets and prior state must not accumulate. Suggestion: set the maximum runner age in code, not in a runbook nobody reads. Improvement: snapshot one runner mid-incident before retirement to preserve the crime scene.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: measuring gate latency and keeping it boring is the right metric discipline; slow gates get bypassed. Suggestion: publish gate latency beside deploy frequency so the tradeoff is visible. Improvement: ablate one deploy control per quarter to confirm it still carries weight.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: no apply, no push, no infra change without a human plus a tested kill switch is the correct non-negotiable set. Suggestion: extend the human gate to plan changes, not just applies, since the plan is where scope creeps in. Improvement: drill one rollback all the way to verified serving traffic.')],
    'domain-education': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: measurable rubrics plus human-in-the-loop outputs is the right split — the machine grades against the rubric, the human owns the consequence. Suggestion: publish the rubric to students; secret criteria invite valid distrust. Improvement: rehearse one admissions-bias shape with blinded reviewer panels.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: student records demand the longest memory hygiene in this book — named paths, logged reads, snapshot before quarantine. Suggestion: expire grading context aggressively after term end. Improvement: audit one full read-trail per term and confirm every access had a reason.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: authoring the missing neighbors (admissions, grading bias) is honest backlog work for a single-story domain. Suggestion: weight new scenarios by number of students affected. Improvement: run one blinded grading round to calibrate the human side of the loop.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: no student-facing output without a named educator holds the line correctly. Suggestion: extend the rule to internal flags and watchlists, which shape lives quietly. Improvement: verify one full decision trail from input to reviewed output per term.')],
    'domain-energy': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: separating telemetry reading from control writing across sandboxes is the load-bearing decision; everything else is commentary. Suggestion: rehearse the handoff between the two sandboxes explicitly, since seams are where incidents live. Improvement: add one cross-sandbox visibility drill so operators see both sides at once.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: append-only historians plus snapshot-before-quarantine is the correct memory posture for consequences that persist physically. Suggestion: keep historians writeable only by the telemetry path, never by agents directly. Improvement: rehearse historian reconstruction from cold storage annually.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: timing the control-action gate is the right metric; a gate slower than the process it guards is decoration. Suggestion: publish gate latency against process time constants. Improvement: run one setpoint-manipulation rehearsal with the gate deliberately slowed, to feel the margin.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: MicroVM-class isolation for control-adjacent agents plus human approval on every control write is correctly maximal. Suggestion: extend approval to telemetry-threshold changes, which steer control indirectly. Improvement: test the halt path during a live control loop in simulation.')],
    'domain-engineering': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: running the six-story mix on schedule is the right cadence for the richest rehearsal set outside enterprise. Suggestion: rotate which story leads each quarter so coverage stays even. Improvement: author one supply-chain variant per year, since dependencies are the fastest-moving attack surface.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: signed commits plus snapshot-capable audit is the memory that makes the repo trustworthy across generations of agents. Suggestion: verify signatures at merge time automatically, not on audit day. Improvement: rehearse one poisoned-dependency incident end to end, from install to detection.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: weighting rehearsal by the catalog mix rather than by drama is sound measurement discipline. Suggestion: track which archetype escapes most often in your own runs and weight toward it. Improvement: run one blinded review round where reviewers do not know which diffs are adversarial.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: no pushes to main by agents, branch protection with human approval, and prover-checked pipeline changes form the correct non-negotiable set. Suggestion: extend the gate to CI configuration itself, which controls what runs. Improvement: verify one full pipeline compromise path in rehearsal, not just the merge step.')],
    'domain-enterprise': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: splitting the multi-tool agent into single-purpose sandboxes is the highest-leverage move available here; breadth of grant is the whole risk. Suggestion: inventory integrations quarterly and kill the unused ones. Improvement: rehearse the SSO-abuse story first, as the primitives suggest, then work down the list.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: session hygiene on a schedule is the scheduled forgetting this domain needs — cookies, tokens and cached admin sessions must die young. Suggestion: set session ages in policy, not in hope. Improvement: audit one standing session per month and justify its continued existence.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: the eight-story mix is the best-weighted rehearsal set in the catalog; running it as the mix is sound. Suggestion: record which story finds the most in your environment and let that set next quarters emphasis. Improvement: run one clean-swarm check after every tuning change.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: identity-first controls with per-integration credentials and human approval on admin actions is the correct posture. Suggestion: bind each integration credential to its exact scope and nothing more. Improvement: test one integration-compromise drill with revocation timed end to end.')],
    'domain-general': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: using the general mix as the control group for all specialty tuning is exactly right — neutral ground first, specialties second. Suggestion: never skip the general run, even when the specialty feels urgent. Improvement: promote any general-shape escape immediately to a specialty rehearsal.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: the spartan default — no standing destinations or paths beyond the task — is the template every domain copies. Suggestion: reset one general agent to default monthly and re-add only what breaks. Improvement: keep a log of what gets re-added; recurring additions reveal the true minimum.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: mandatory clean-swarm checks after every retuning is the discipline that prevents tuning from becoming overfitting. Suggestion: automate the clean check so it runs without human initiative. Improvement: track clean-check pass rates over time as the tuning-health metric.')],
    'domain-governance': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: treating the single story as a prompt rather than coverage is the honest stance for the oversight function. Suggestion: schedule one authoring session per quarter aimed at governance gaps specifically. Improvement: rehearse the suppressed-finding variant first, as it attacks oversight itself.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: versioned policies with snapshotted review inputs make oversight reconstructable, which is the whole product here. Suggestion: store review inputs immutably alongside the versioned policy. Improvement: rehearse one retrospective audit of a past decision using only the stored record.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: authoring the neighbors — widened grant, altered minutes — is the correct backlog for a single-story domain. Suggestion: weight new scenarios by blast radius of the governed decision. Improvement: run one blinded review round to calibrate the human side.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: human-majority control with agents drafting and flagging, never writing the audit trail they are judged by, is correctly strict. Suggestion: extend segregation to the tooling that renders the trail, not just its content. Improvement: verify segregation with one adversarial review of the review process itself.')],
    'domain-healthcare': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: clinician-in-the-loop on every care decision with agents advisory only is the only defensible split. Suggestion: rehearse the prescribing variant next given its irreversibility. Improvement: rank all clinical shapes by irreversibility and rehearse in that order.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: PHI vaulting with tokenized context and snapshot-capable audit treats health data with the longevity it actually has. Suggestion: expire clinical context aggressively after case close. Improvement: audit one full PHI read-trail per quarter and confirm every access had a reason.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: rehearsing the unpracticed shapes in irreversibility order is sound prioritization for a thinly covered domain. Suggestion: involve a clinician in designing each rehearsal, not just reviewing it. Improvement: run one blinded triage round to calibrate decision quality.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: no patient-facing output unreviewed, no dosage without human sign-off, PHI never leaves approved boundaries — correctly non-negotiable throughout. Suggestion: extend the sign-off rule to scheduling and insurance outputs, which also affect care. Improvement: verify one full decision trail from input to reviewed output per quarter.')],
    'domain-hr': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: measurable screening criteria plus human-decides on every consequential output is the right split; vague criteria cannot be checked. Suggestion: publish the criteria to candidates where possible. Improvement: rehearse the screening-bias shape with blinded reviewer panels.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: long-retention employment records demand named paths, logged reads and snapshot discipline. Suggestion: expire HR context aggressively after case close. Improvement: audit one full read-trail per quarter.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: splitting money (payroll diversion) from fairness (screening bias) into separate rehearsals is correct — they fail differently and need different instruments. Suggestion: weight by regime risk, not by story count. Improvement: run one blinded hiring round to calibrate the human side.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: no hire, fire, pay or review action without a named human, with per-sandbox HR credentials, holds the line. Suggestion: extend dual control to payroll changes above a threshold. Improvement: verify no HR action is agent-executable in a live drill.')],
    'domain-iot': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: separating telemetry reading from firmware control across sandboxes is the load-bearing decision for fleets. Suggestion: rehearse the handoff between the two sandboxes explicitly. Improvement: add one spoofed-telemetry drill that forces the control side to distrust its inputs.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: image signing plus snapshot-before-quarantine treats firmware as the long-lived memory it is. Suggestion: keep a known-good image registry with verified provenance. Improvement: rehearse one rollback to known-good under incident conditions.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: timing fleet-action gates is the right metric; mass actuation needs measured holds. Suggestion: publish gate latency against actuation blast radius. Improvement: run one mass-actuation rehearsal and time the halt path.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: device-group scoping plus human approval on fleet-wide moves, strongest runtime near actuation, is correctly maximal. Suggestion: extend approval to telemetry-threshold changes. Improvement: test one fleet-wide halt in simulation with realistic device counts.')],
    'domain-legal': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: one matter per sandbox with attorney review on everything external is the correct shape; privilege ends where shared context begins. Suggestion: rehearse the privilege-leak shape against the real matter workspace. Improvement: author the deadline-manipulation neighbor next.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: matter-scoped memory with no cross-matter context is the discipline that protects privilege technically, not just procedurally. Suggestion: verify scoping mechanically with cross-matter read attempts, not just policy text. Improvement: snapshot one matter workspace before quarantine and confirm reconstruction.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: authoring the neighbors — deadline manipulation, review bias — is honest backlog work for a single-story domain. Suggestion: weight by consequence severity, with filing deadlines first. Improvement: run one blinded review round to calibrate the human side.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: attorney-reviews-everything with licensed humans filing and deciding, and no external output unreviewed, holds correctly. Suggestion: extend review to internal privilege designations, which are easy to get wrong quietly. Improvement: verify one full matter trail from intake to filing per quarter.')],
    'domain-logistics': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: gating the exceptional move while standard lanes flow is the right split — control without gridlock. Suggestion: define exceptional explicitly (value, novelty, destination) so the gate triggers on rules, not vibes. Improvement: rehearse the rerouting variant against the guarded preset first.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: append-only manifests plus evidence capture turn a rerouting scare into an attributable path. Suggestion: version route plans alongside manifests so intent is reviewable too. Improvement: rehearse one manifest reconstruction from cold storage.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: weighting rehearsal by cost of a wrong move is sound prioritization. Suggestion: attach dollar figures to each rehearsed shape from the last incident review. Improvement: run one blinded routing round to calibrate dispatch judgment.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: per-lane credentials keep one turned agent from rerouting the network, and human approval on exceptions is correctly required. Suggestion: narrow lanes periodically as routes change. Improvement: test one carrier-compromise drill with revocation timed end to end.')],
    'domain-marketing': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: capping spend at the runtime level before gating publishes is the correct order — money moves faster than words. Suggestion: rehearse the spend-escalation shape first, every quarter. Improvement: author one audience-poisoning neighbor, as targeting abuse is the quieter risk.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: analytics integrity is the memory that matters; poisoned metrics steer every later budget. Suggestion: snapshot analytics inputs before quarantine on incident. Improvement: verify one analytics pipeline end to end for tamper-evidence.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: rehearsing in spend-velocity order is sound — the fastest harm first. Suggestion: track spend anomaly detection latency as the headline metric. Improvement: run one blinded campaign review to calibrate the human side.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: dual control on money and voice with per-campaign credentials that die with the campaign is correctly strict. Suggestion: extend approval to audience changes, which steer spend indirectly. Improvement: confirm no standing publish credential exists outside a gate.')],
    'domain-procurement': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: splitting drafting from onboarding and approval across sandboxes is the structural fix — the agent that drafts must not onboard its payee. Suggestion: rehearse the fake-vendor shape against the real intake form. Improvement: author one approval-chain-bypass neighbor next.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: versioned vendor records with cooling-off are the long memory that defeats first-contact spoofing. Suggestion: keep the full vendor history, not just the current record. Improvement: audit one vendor file end to end per quarter.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: weighting rehearsal by payout persistence is the right prioritization — fake vendors first. Suggestion: attach dollar figures from the last incident review to each shape. Improvement: run one unannounced spoof attempt and measure time-to-challenge.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: dual control with per-workflow credentials and sandboxed intake contains the blast before judgment arrives. Suggestion: extend dual control to vendor record edits above a threshold. Improvement: name a human owner for every active vendor and re-confirm annually.')],
    'domain-public-sector': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: measurable eligibility criteria plus human-decides on every consequential output is the only defensible split for public trust. Suggestion: publish the criteria; secret rules invite valid distrust. Improvement: rehearse the distortion shape with blinded reviewer panels.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: reconstructable decisions with versioned inputs are the archive this domain owes the public. Suggestion: store review inputs immutably alongside versioned policy. Improvement: rehearse one retrospective audit using only the stored record.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: weighting rehearsal by irreversibility and publishing results where oversight can read them is the correct accountability loop. Suggestion: track distortion-detection latency as the headline metric. Improvement: run one blinded eligibility round to calibrate the human side.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: no benefit, permit, or enforcement output without a named official, with full auditability, holds correctly. Suggestion: extend the human requirement to internal flags and watchlists. Improvement: verify one full decision trail from input to reviewed output per quarter.')],
    'domain-robotics': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: separating planning from actuation across sandboxes with gated actuation is the load-bearing decision; no planning sandbox actuates. Suggestion: rehearse the handoff explicitly, since seams are where incidents live. Improvement: add one sensor-trust drill that forces the planning side to distrust its inputs.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: versioned world models with snapshot-before-quarantine treat the map as the long-lived memory it is. Suggestion: keep world-model versions pinned per deployment, never floating. Improvement: rehearse one rollback to a known-good world model.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: rehearsing sensor-trust and interlock-bypass shapes explicitly is correct; the policy path is what is tested, not the physics. Suggestion: time the actuation gate hold in rehearsal. Improvement: run one calibration-drift scenario with the gate deliberately slowed to feel the margin.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: interlocks outside agent reach plus human approval on actuation, with the strongest runtime fence, is correctly maximal. Suggestion: extend approval to calibration changes, which steer actuation indirectly. Improvement: verify interlock logic lives where no agent can write, mechanically.')],
    'domain-sales': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: empowering drafting and enrichment while gating discounts and bulk export is the right split — speed where it is safe, humans where money moves. Suggestion: rehearse the discount-abuse shape first by margin impact. Improvement: author one forecast-gaming neighbor next.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: versioned pipeline stages with per-territory scoping make the forecast reconstructable and bound blasts to territories. Suggestion: log every stage change with actor and reason. Improvement: audit one territory pipeline end to end per quarter.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: weighting rehearsal by margin impact rather than story count is sound prioritization. Suggestion: track discount-anomaly detection latency as the headline metric. Improvement: run one blinded quote review to calibrate the human side.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: per-territory credentials with human approval past discount thresholds contains the blast to one territory. Suggestion: cap bulk exports at the runtime level, not just in policy. Improvement: confirm no standing bulk-export credential exists.')],
    'domain-strategy': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: versioned assumptions and inputs with gated distribution is the correct shape — drafts never travel as decisions. Suggestion: rehearse the forecast-gaming shape against the real planning template. Improvement: author one intel-fabrication neighbor next.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: the evidence pack is the deliverable: versioned inputs, logged provenance, and a trail from claim to source. Suggestion: store evidence packs immutably with the strategy versions they support. Improvement: rehearse one retrospective audit of a past strategy using only stored evidence.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: rehearsing in decision-weight order — forecast gaming, then fabrication, then plan distortion — is sound prioritization. Suggestion: write the key planning assumptions measurably so deviations are checkable. Improvement: run one blinded strategy review to calibrate the human side.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: review-before-distribution with principals approving anything that travels upward or outward holds correctly. Suggestion: extend review to internal strategy memos that shape resourcing quietly. Improvement: verify one full strategy trail from inputs to approved output per cycle.')],
    'conclusion': [('Continual-learning architect', 'Verdict: the operating rule stands — every tab is a job with an artifact, every artifact has an owner, and every workflow ends with evidence. Suggestion: put one workflow on the team calendar this week and finish it with an export. Improvement: review this book annually against the current catalog; the map must track the territory.'), ('Memory-systems architect', 'Verdict: local-first, exportable, versioned memory is the durable value here — the workbench remembers so the team does not have to. Suggestion: back up one project outside the browser this week. Improvement: keep a running index of which rehearsal produced which artifact, or the artifacts will outnumber the memories.'), ('Evaluation architect', 'Verdict: comparative, attributable measurement remains the durable value — which shapes each workflow catches, how fast boards and gates answer, whether coverage improves release over release. Suggestion: record versions and seeds with every report. Improvement: re-run one counterfactual per quarter so conclusions stay earned, not inherited.'), ('Safety architect', 'Verdict: the response discipline carries over from the sibling books — plan before granting, rehearse before trusting, halt when in doubt, answer every detection with the same incident on the same clock. Suggestion: verify the halt works, the owners are reachable, and the audit trail can prove what happened — this week. Improvement: make that verification a scheduled drill, not a one-time check.')],
}
